  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nHOT AND DRY THIS WEEKEND INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nHigh pressure will strengthen and anchor itself across the\nDesert Southwest this weekend, reinforcing this drier air mass\nand allowing temperatures to climb a bit higher well into the\ntriple digits for the valleys of eastern Utah and lower western\nColorado. We will be under a northerly flow and a more stable\nregime, so it will be pretty hard pressed to get much convection\nor shower activity going. Storm activity seems to favor areas\neast of the divide, with the eastern and far southern San Juans\nstanding the best chance of isolated storms potentially \ndrifting south off the terrain Saturday afternoon and again on \nSunday afternoon. Given the much drier air mass, lack of upper\nlevel support and lack of forcing...do not expect any activity\nto last very long and will be more conducive to gusty outflow\nwinds and lightning rather than any wetting rain. Regarding the\nheat, record high temperatures are possible for many locations\nwith low relative humidity in the teens and single digits for\nthe lower valleys, giving us a bit of a reprieve from the\nmonsoonal moisture through at least early next week. On Monday,\na trough will track across the Northern Rockies into the Plains,\nwhich could introduce some clouds and a brief suppression of the\nhigh pressure. It appears like storms would favor areas east of\nthe divide while the western slope remains hot and dry, but the\nhigher terrain of the divide could see some isolated afternoon\nstorm activity. Extreme Heat Warnings and Heat Advisories remain\nin effect this weekend through Monday for our eastern Utah\nvalleys and Grand Valley in west-central Colorado. See product\nfor details and exact timing for those particular locations. \n\nMOISTURE POTENTIAL MID TO LATE NEXT WEEK:\n\nLooking ahead in the week, the pattern becomes a bit\ndisorganized with the high pressure remaining across the Desert\nSouthwest and Great Basin but becoming a bit squashed and not as\nstrong mid to late next week. It appears that we will slowly\nintroduce some moisture across the Four Corners as it seeps in\nunderneath this ridge from Arizona and New Mexico. This will\nallow for a bit more afternoon thunderstorm development over the\nsouthern mountains at first with that moisture and storm\ncoverage potentially slowly creeping northward by late next\nweek. The northern stream will also become more active with a\nfew passing shortwaves across the northern tier of states that\ncould help provide the needed forcing. This is a low confidence\nforecast on this moisture return and extent of storm activity.\nOtherwise, temperatures look to remain hot around 10 degrees\nabove normal.",
